 squares (like C-squares) prematurely.\n\nEvaluation: \n- **(C, 1)**: This is a poor move. It places a piece on a C-square (adjacent to a corner) and flips a whole row (C2, C3, C4). This creates a large frontier and gives the opponent easy access to the top-left corner (A1) later in the game.\n- **(F, 3) and (F, 5)**: These moves extend towards the outer edges of the board. While playable, they are less optimal than keeping the play tight in the center 4x4 squares.\n- **(B, 3)**: A reasonable move, but it is slightly further out than D3.\n- **(D, 3)**: This is the strongest candidate. It places a piece in the central 4x4 box. It flips D4 (which becomes my piece), connecting D3 to my existing piece at D5. The result is a compact horizontal line of pieces (D3, D4, D5) supported by C5. This is a "quiet move" that limits the opponent\'s immediate counter-play options.\n\nReason: Move (D, 3) is excellent for centralizing control. It flips an internal piece (D4) and creates a stable block of friendly pieces in the center of the board. It avoids opening up the edges or creating a large number of weak disks that the opponent can easily flip back, maintaining a positional advantage.\n\nAnswer: (D, 3)'}
