on: The game is in the early-to-mid stage. I (@) control a central cluster of pieces, while the opponent (*) has presence on the upper and left sides, including some outer layers. My goal is to make a "quiet move" that flips minimal pieces and consolidates my position without creating a large "frontier" of pieces that the opponent can easily attack.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Moves like **(A, 3)**, **(C, 1)**, and **(H, 2)** are edge moves. Taking edges this early without corner security is risky and often allows the opponent to force flips or gain access to corners.\n- Moves like **(B, 2)** and **(G, 7)** are X-squares (adjacent to corners diagonally), which are extremely dangerous as they typically surrender the corner to the opponent.\n- **(B, 5)** flips C4. C4 is adjacent to several opponent pieces, meaning flipping it creates a new frontier that the opponent can exploit.\n- **(F, 3)** is a central, non-edge move. It flips the opponent\'s piece at F4 vertically (F3 -> F4 -> F5).\n\nReason: \n(F, 3) is the strongest move because it is a "quiet move," flipping only a single disc (F4). Crucially, the flipped disc at F4 becomes an internal piece, surrounded by my existing pieces at E3, E4, E5, and F5. This means I am not opening up new attack lines for the opponent. It strengthens my central control while restricting the opponent\'s mobility.\n\nAnswer: (F, 3)'}
