onent lacks the positioning to flip it back immediately. It is a "quiet" edge move that strengthens my presence on the right side.\n2.  **(A, 3)**: This is another edge move. However, it flips four pieces (B3, C3, D3, B4). While it secures an edge, flipping many internal pieces can often open up new valid moves for the opponent, which is strategically risky.\n3.  **(D, 1)** and **(E, 1)**: These moves on the left edge are risky because the opponent has a piece at F1. Playing here could allow the opponent to wedge in and flip my edge pieces, particularly E1 which is vulnerable to a D1 response.\n4.  **(F, 7)**: A quiet internal move, but taking a stable edge like C8 is generally worth more value.\n\nReason: I choose (C, 8) because it secures a stable piece on the edge of the board. It flips fewer pieces (2) compared to the other viable edge move at (A, 3) (which flips 4), thereby minimizing the new options given to the opponent. It does not immediately endanger the H8 corner and removes some of the opponent\'s central control.\n\nAnswer: (C, 8)'}
