odical assessment of the best options and a review of all the moves in relation to the current state, H3 is the best move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I (@) hold a presence in the center and bottom-right, while the opponent (*) dominates the top-left block. Several edges (A, H, 1) are largely open. The goal is to maximize stability and minimize the opponent\'s mobility (avoiding giving them easy moves).\n\nEvaluation: \n1.  **(H, 3)**: This is a "quiet" edge move. It captures only one piece (G3) by sandwiching it against F3. It does not open the H1 corner to the opponent; in fact, if the opponent plays the C-square at H2 to attack, I can respond by taking the H1 corner.\n2.  **(A, 3)**: An edge move that captures three pieces (B3, C3, D3). While it sets up a potential corner trap at A1, flipping three pieces increases the opponent\'s mobility significantly compared to H3.\n3.  **(C, 1) / (E, 1)**: These edge moves allow the opponent to wedge in at D1, which could be disadvantageous.\n4.  **(H, 5)**: This move allows the opponent to play H6, which could eventually lead to them forcing the H8 corner.\n5.  **(D, 1)**: Flips almost the entire D-row. This is too "loud" (flips too many discs), giving the opponent massive mobility and access to the bottom area.\n\nReason: **(H, 3)** is the best move because it is the most stable and "quiet" option available. It secures a position on the edge while flipping only a single disc, thereby restricting the opponent\'s future valid moves. It also sets a tactical trap: it prevents the opponent from easily taking the edge and penalizes them with the loss of the H1 corner if they attempt to play adjacent to it at H2.\n\nAnswer: (H, 3)'}
